<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theodore II Laskaris</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1221">1221</a>–<a href="/years/1258">1258</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Theodore II Laskaris was emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> from 1254 to 1258, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-doukas-vatatzes">John III Doukas Vatatzes</a> and grandson of Theodore I. A pupil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-blemmydes">Nikephoros Blemmydes</a>, he was a scholar and writer of theological and philosophical works. As emperor he defended Nicaean lands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and relied on officials of modest birth rather than the aristocracy. He suffered from epilepsy and died young.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theodore-ii-laskaris.jpg" alt="Theodore II Laskaris" width="320" height="629" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Pachymeres · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Theodoros_II_Laskaris.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Theodore was born in the imperial palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>, the only child of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-doukas-vatatzes">John III Doukas Vatatzes</a> and Irene Laskarina, eldest daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-i-laskaris">Theodore I Laskaris</a>, who had founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-nicaea">Empire of Nicaea</a> after the crusaders captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1204. He adopted his mother's family name. He studied under two leading scholars, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-blemmydes">Nikephoros Blemmydes</a> and George Akropolites, learning grammar, poetry, rhetoric, logic, mathematics, astronomy, geometry and music, and he began writing treatises on theological, historical and philosophical themes in his youth.</p><p>In 1235 he married Elena Asenina to secure an alliance with her father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-ii">Ivan Asen II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-empire">Latin Empire</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. From 1241 he acted as his father's lieutenant in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, and from about 1242 he was a co-ruler though never crowned. He formed a close circle of young intellectuals, above all George Mouzalon, a page of low birth, while his relations with aristocrats such as Theodore Philes and Michael Palaiologos grew tense.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Theodore succeeded his father on 4 November 1254 and was crowned by Patriarch Arsenios Autoreianos on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> 1254. He dismissed many high officials and army commanders of aristocratic origin and replaced them with loyal friends, some of low birth. He allied defensively with the Seljuk Sultan of Rum, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaykaus-ii">Kaykaus II</a>, against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, and reformed the army by recruiting more soldiers from the peasantry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>.</p><p>The Bulgarian tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-ii-asen">Michael II Asen</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and Macedonia in the winter of 1254 to 1255, and Theodore led the counter-attack himself, forcing the invaders out of Thrace. He later made peace, and in 1256 he forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-ii-komnenos-doukas">Michael II Komnenos Doukas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> to cede Servia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Dyrrachium</a>, securing control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-egnatia">Via Egnatia</a>. Michael II then allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-uros-i">Stefan Uroš I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manfred-king-of-sicily">Manfred of Sicily</a> against him. His newly appointed generals could not resist their joint invasion in 1257. Negotiations with a papal legate on church union proved fruitless.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Theodore fell seriously ill in November 1257. George Pachymeres diagnosed epilepsy, while Akropolites noticed extreme weight loss and Blemmydes called it a strange disease. Modern historians of medicine have not agreed on a diagnosis. He died on 16 August 1258, of either chronic epilepsy or cancer, having appointed George Mouzalon regent for his underage son John IV.</p><p>Within ten days Mouzalon fell victim to an aristocratic plot, and Michael Palaiologos assumed the regency, soon usurping the throne. The patriarchal appointment of Arsenios, whom hostile sources called Theodore's sole nominee, later gave rise to the Arsenite Schism, which lasted from 1265 to 1310.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Theodore II Laskaris” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>George Mouzalon, a high official of the Empire of Nicaea under <b>Theodore II Laskaris</b> and its regent, was assassinated in a conspiracy of nobles led by Michael VIII Palaiologos, who became co-emperor in 1259 and recovered Constantinople in 1261.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-25">25 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Scholarship flourished under Nikephoros Blemmydes and <b>Theodore II Laskaris</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/empire-of-nicaea">Empire of Nicaea</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in İznik, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in İznik, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.43,29.72&amp;year=1245" aria-label="İznik on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5280 719 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Theodore II Laskaris"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5730 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5730 989h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.43,29.72&amp;year=1245">What was here in 1245? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
